<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; max-width: 800px; margin: auto;">

    <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
        <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Local Farm Stand</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Much Are the Bananas?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify specific information in announcements or recordings. Interactive: Engage in simple discussions on lessons or current events.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify specific items and their prices from announcements in the market (e.g., "Two papayas for three dollars." "This papaya costs two dollars."). Can engage in simple discussions about items bought at the market (e.g., "I buy bananas."). Can respond to questions about prices and quantities in role-play scenarios (e.g., "How much is the banana?" "It is six bananas for one dollar.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or realia of various fruits and vegetables (e.g., banana, papaya, mango, tomato, lettuce, pineapple, pepper) and asks, "What is this?" and "Where can we buy this?" to elicit "market" or "farm stand." Students shout out names they know. Teacher introduces "farm stand" if not mentioned.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of new vocabulary words related to the farm stand (e.g., papaya, banana, mango, pineapple, tomato, lettuce). The teacher specifically emphasizes the /p/ sound in "papaya" and the /b/ sound in "banana" by repeating them slowly and clearly. The teacher also claps out syllables for "banana" (ba-na-na) and "papaya" (pa-pa-ya) and asks students to imitate. The teacher models the question "How much is this?" while pointing to items, clarifying its meaning through gestures and simple examples.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a mango) "Is a mango a fruit or a vegetable?" (Expected answer: "Fruit.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(After modeling "How much is this?") "Do we ask 'how much' to know the color or the price?" (Expected answer: "The price.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio recording (20-30 seconds) of a cashier announcing prices at a farm stand. Example phrases in the recording: "Hello! Welcome to the Fresh Farm Stand! Today, delicious bananas, six for one dollar! Fresh papayas, two dollars each! We have some sweet mangoes, three dollars each. And tasty tomatoes, one dollar each. Thank you for buying fresh!"</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify specific information in announcements or recordings.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (first listen):</b> "What place do you hear?" "What are they selling?" (Students listen and identify the general context.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Specific information questions (second listen):</b> The teacher asks students to listen for specific items and prices. "How many bananas for one dollar?" "How much is one papaya?" "How much are the mangoes?" "What other vegetable do you hear?" (Students raise hands or write down answers.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> WH-questions ("How much is...?"), "some" and "any".</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> mango, pineapple, papaya, banana, tomato, pepper, lettuce, fruit, vegetable, price.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /p/ in "papaya", /b/ in "banana"; segmenting syllables.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure (Listening-focused activities):</b></p>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: Listen and Fill-in-the-Blanks:</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the audio recording containing blanks. The teacher plays the audio again, and students listen carefully to fill in the missing item names or prices. E.g., "Delicious __________, six for one dollar!" "Fresh __________, two dollars each!" "We have _________ sweet mangoes."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: Listen and Point/Match:</b> The teacher calls out a fruit/vegetable and a price (e.g., "pineapple, four dollars"). Students listen and point to the correct picture on their worksheet or flashcard. For pronunciation practice, the teacher says pairs like "big papaya / big banana," and students listen and raise their hand for "papaya" or "banana" based on the sound they hear. The teacher also says words like "tomato," and students clap the syllables (to-ma-to).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check: Listen and Verify:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of item pictures with written prices (e.g., a banana with "$1" written next to it). Student A reads a price and item (e.g., "The mango is three dollars."), and Student B listens and checks if the pronunciation and information match the picture. They switch roles. The teacher circulates, listening for accurate comprehension and pronunciation.</li>
                    </ol>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15-18 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play in pairs.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "Farm Stand Shopper & Cashier"</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. One student is the "Farm Stand Cashier" and the other is the "Shopper."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "Cashier" has cards with pictures of fruits/vegetables and their prices (e.g., "papaya: $2," "banana: 6 for $1," "lettuce: $1").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "Shopper" approaches the stand and asks: "Hello! Can you help me, please? How much are the bananas?" (Polite request, WH-question).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "Cashier" responds, pronouncing clearly: "The bananas are six for one dollar."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "Shopper" then asks about other items, e.g., "Do you have any mangoes?" The "Cashier" responds: "Yes, we have some mangoes. They are three dollars each."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on the Shopper's ability to listen and understand the prices and quantities, and the Cashier's ability to clearly state them, reinforcing the target vocabulary and grammar in a realistic interaction. Students switch roles after a few transactions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[8-10 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play in Stage 4.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Farm Stand Shopper & Cashier" role-play.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Listening Focus):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students (especially the Cashier) pronounce target words (e.g., papaya, banana) clearly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students use new vocabulary (e.g., mango, pineapple, price) in context.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Comprehension:</b> The Shopper clearly understands the prices and items announced by the Cashier (evidenced by appropriate responses or actions, like picking up the correct item).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Students interact effectively and politely in the role-play.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete these prompts before leaving.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can understand the price of (name one fruit/vegetable) when someone tells me."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word I heard and understood today?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to hear and understand today when we talked about the farm stand?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult to understand or hear clearly?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in the listening activities? (e.g., student engagement with audio, successful identification of prices/items).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students struggling with listening comprehension (e.g., slower audio, more visual aids, repetition, pre-teaching numbers)?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How did students manage the pronunciation focus on /p/ and /b/ sounds?</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Great job listening to the prices and names of fruits and vegetables! Next time, we will read about a farm stand and find the same words to understand more about buying food." (Connects to Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension, using the same vocabulary.)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[2-3 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>